🔹 What is useEffect?
👉 useEffect tells React to do something after the screen is shown or updated.

🧠 One-line meaning
useEffect is used to run extra work after rendering a component.

🔹 Why do we need useEffect?
Some things are not UI:

API calls

Timers

Event listeners

Logging

These are called side effects.

👉 useEffect is used to handle them.

🔹 Simple example
useEffect(() => {
  console.log("Component loaded");
}, []);
Meaning:

Run this code once

After the component appears

🔁 When does useEffect run?
1️⃣ Run once
useEffect(() => {}, []);
👉 After first render

2️⃣ Run when value changes
useEffect(() => {}, [count]);
👉 Runs when count changes

3️⃣ Run every time
useEffect(() => {});
👉 Runs after every render

🧠 Real-life example
Turning on a fan after entering a room

🔹 Cleanup (simple)
useEffect can also clean up:

useEffect(() => {
  return () => {
    console.log("Component removed");
  };
}, []);
Meaning:

Stop timer

Remove listeners

💡 Like turning off fan when leaving room

🧾 Interview-ready answer
useEffect is a React hook used to perform side effects such as API calls or subscriptions after a component renders.

🧠 Super short summary
useEffect = after-render work

Used for side effects

Runs based on dependencies

